## sn21.3
sn21.3:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn21.3:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn21.3:1.3 | S: At that time the venerables Sāriputta and Moggallāna were staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: And on that occasion Ven. Sāriputta & Ven. Mahā Moggallāna were staying near Rājagaha in a single dwelling in the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
sn21.3:1.4 | S: Then in the late afternoon, Venerable Sāriputta came out of retreat, went to Venerable Moggallāna, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Sāriputta, arising from his seclusion in the late afternoon, went to Ven. Mahā Moggallāna. On arrival, he exchanged courteous greetings with him.
sn21.3:1.5 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, Sāriputta sat down to one side, and said to Mahāmoggallāna: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Mahā Moggallāna,
sn21.3:2.1 | S: “Reverend Moggallāna, your faculties are so very clear, and your complexion is pure and bright. | T: “Bright are your faculties, friend Moggallāna; pure your complexion, and clear.
sn21.3:2.2 | S: Have you spent the day in a peaceful meditation?” | T: Could it be that Ven. Mahā Moggallāna has spent today in a peaceful abiding?”
sn21.3:2.3 | S: “Reverend, I’ve spent the day in a coarse meditation. | T: “It was in a gross abiding, my friend, that I spent today.
sn21.3:2.4 | S: But I have had some Dhamma talk.” | T: But I had some Dhamma talk.”
sn21.3:2.5 | S: “Who did you have a Dhamma talk with?” | T: “With whom did Ven. Moggallāna have some Dhamma talk?”
sn21.3:2.6 | S: “With the Buddha.” | T: “With the Blessed One, my friend.”
sn21.3:2.7 | S: “But Reverend, the Buddha is far away. He’s staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: “But far away is the Blessed One now, in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn21.3:2.8 | S: Did you go to him with your psychic power, | T: Did Ven. Mahā Moggallāna go to the Blessed One through psychic power,
sn21.3:2.9 | S: or did he come to you?” | T: or did the Blessed One come to Ven. Mahā Moggallāna through psychic power?”
sn21.3:2.10 | S: “No reverend, I didn’t go to him with my psychic power, | T: “I didn’t go to the Blessed One through psychic power, my friend,
sn21.3:2.11 | S: nor did he come to me. | T: nor did the Blessed One come to me through psychic power.
sn21.3:2.12 | S: Rather, the Buddha cleared his clairvoyance and clairaudience toward me, | T: Simply that the Blessed One purified his divine eye & divine ear as far as me, and I purified my divine eye & divine ear as far as the Blessed One.”
sn21.3:2.13 | S: and I cleared my clairvoyance and clairaudience toward him.” | T: (none)
sn21.3:2.14 | S: “But what manner of Dhamma talk did you have together?” | T: “And what kind of Dhamma talk did Ven. Mahā Moggallāna have with the Blessed One?”
sn21.3:3.1 | S: “Well, reverend, I said to the Buddha, | T: “Just now, my friend, I said to the Blessed One,
sn21.3:3.2 | S: ‘Sir, they speak of one who is energetic. | T: ‘One of aroused persistence, one of aroused persistence,’ it is said, lord.
sn21.3:3.3 | S: How is an energetic person defined?’ | T: To what extent is a person one of aroused persistence?’
sn21.3:3.4 | S: When I said this, the Buddha said, | T: When this was said, the Blessed One said,
sn21.3:3.5 | S: ‘Moggallāna, it’s when a mendicant lives with energy roused up: | T: ‘There is the case, Moggallāna, where a monk, (thinking,)
sn21.3:3.6 | S: “Gladly, let only skin, sinews, and bones remain! Let the flesh and blood waste away in my body! I will not stop trying until I have achieved what is possible by human strength, energy, and vigor.” | T: “Gladly would I let the flesh & blood in my body dry up, leaving just the skin, tendons, & bones, but if I have not attained what can be reached through manly firmness, manly persistence, manly striving, there will be no relaxing my persistence”:
sn21.3:3.7 | S: That’s how an individual is energetic.’ | T: That is how one is a person of aroused persistence.’
sn21.3:3.8 | S: That’s the Dhamma talk I had together with the Buddha.” | T: That is the Dhamma talk I had with the Blessed One, my friend.”
sn21.3:4.1 | S: “Reverend, next to Venerable Mahāmoggallāna I’m like a few pieces of gravel next to the Himalayas, the king of mountains. | T: “Friend, like a few small pieces of gravel placed next to the Himalayas, the king of mountains,
