## sn21.6
sn21.6:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
sn21.6:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Bhaddiya the Dwarf went to the Buddha. | T: And on that occasion Ven. Bhaddiya the Dwarf, following behind a large number of monks, was going to the Blessed One.
sn21.6:1.3 | S: The Buddha saw him coming off in the distance, | T: From afar, the Blessed One saw Ven. Bhaddiya the Dwarf coming, following behind a large number of monks: ugly, unsightly, stunted, treated with condescension by most of the monks.
sn21.6:1.4 | S: and addressed the mendicants: | T: On seeing him, the Blessed One addressed the monks,
sn21.6:1.5 | S: “Mendicants, do you see this monk coming—ugly, unsightly, deformed, and despised by the mendicants?” | T: “Monks, do you see that monk coming from afar, following behind a large number of monks: ugly, unsightly, stunted, treated with condescension by most of the monks?”
sn21.6:1.6 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
sn21.6:1.7 | S: “That mendicant is very mighty and powerful. It’s not easy to find an attainment that he has not already attained. | T: “That, monks, is a monk of great power, great might. The attainment already attained by that monk is not of a sort easily attained.
sn21.6:1.8 | S: And he has realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lives having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness.” | T: And by means of it he has entered & remains in the supreme goal of the holy life for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, directly knowing & realizing it for himself right in the here & now.”
sn21.6:1.9 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: That is what the Blessed One said.
sn21.6:1.10 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said that, the One Well-Gone, the Teacher, said further:
sn21.6:2.1 | S: “Geese, herons, and peacocks, | T: Swans, cranes, & peacocks,
sn21.6:2.2 | S: elephants and spotted deer— | T: elephants & spotted antelope
sn21.6:2.3 | S: though their bodies are not equal, | T: all fear the lion (though) in body there’s no comparison.
sn21.6:2.4 | S: they all fear the lion. | T: (none)
sn21.6:3.1 | S: So it is for humans— | T: In the same way, among human beings,
sn21.6:3.2 | S: if a little person is wise, | T: even if one is small but endowed in discernment,
sn21.6:3.3 | S: they’re the truly great one, | T: one is great for that—
sn21.6:3.4 | S: not the fool with a good body.” | T: not the fool endowed in physique.
sn21.6:3.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.53
sn22.53:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī. There the Blessed One said,
sn22.53:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, if you get involved, you’re not free. If you’re not involved, you’re free. | T: “One attached is unreleased; one unattached is released.
sn22.53:1.3 | S: Should consciousness involved with form, supported by form, established on form continue to remain, with a sprinkling of relishing it would grow, increase, and mature. | T: Should consciousness, when standing, stand attached to (a physical) form, supported by form (as its object), landing on form, watered with delight, it would exhibit growth, increase, & proliferation.
sn22.53:1.4 | S: Or should consciousness involved with feeling remain … | T: “Should consciousness, when standing, stand attached to feeling, supported by feeling (as its object), landing on feeling, watered with delight, it would exhibit growth, increase, & proliferation.
sn22.53:1.5 | S: Or should consciousness involved with perception remain … | T: “Should consciousness, when standing, stand attached to perception, supported by perception (as its object), landing on perception, watered with delight, it would exhibit growth, increase, & proliferation.
sn22.53:1.6 | S: Or should consciousness involved with choices, supported by choices, established on choices continue to remain, with a sprinkling of relishing it would grow, increase, and mature. | T: “Should consciousness, when standing, stand attached to fabrications, supported by fabrications (as its object), landing on fabrications, watered with delight, it would exhibit growth, increase, & proliferation.
sn22.53:2.1 | S: Mendicants, suppose you say: | T: “Were someone to say,
sn22.53:2.2 | S: ‘Apart from form, feeling, perception, and choices, I will describe the coming and going of consciousness, its passing away and reappearing, its growth, increase, and maturity.’ That is not possible. | T: ‘I will describe a coming, a going, a passing away, an arising, a growth, an increase, or a proliferation of consciousness apart from form, from feeling, from perception, from fabrications,’ that would be impossible.
sn22.53:3.1 | S: If a mendicant has given up greed for the form element, | T: “If a monk abandons passion for the property of form.…
sn22.53:3.2 | S: the support is cut off, and there is no establishment of consciousness. | T: (none)
sn22.53:3.3 | S: If a mendicant has given up greed for the feeling element … | T: “If a monk abandons passion for the property of feeling.…
sn22.53:3.4 | S: perception element … | T: “If a monk abandons passion for the property of perception.…
sn22.53:3.5 | S: choices element … | T: “If a monk abandons passion for the property of fabrications.…
sn22.53:3.6 | S: consciousness element, | T: “If a monk abandons passion for the property of consciousness,
sn22.53:3.7 | S: the support is cut off, and there is no establishment of consciousness. | T: then owing to the abandonment of passion, the support is cut off, and there is no landing of consciousness.
sn22.53:3.8 | S: Since that consciousness does not become established and does not grow, not making choices, it is freed. | T: Consciousness, thus not having landed, not increasing, not concocting, is released.
sn22.53:3.9 | S: Being free, it’s stable. Being stable, it’s content. Being content, they’re not anxious. Not being anxious, they personally become extinguished. | T: Owing to its release, it is steady. Owing to its steadiness, it is contented. Owing to its contentment, it is not agitated. Not agitated, he (the monk) is totally unbound right within.
sn22.53:3.10 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: He discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’”
sn22.53:3.11 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn7.17
sn7.17:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Kosalans in a certain forest grove. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Kosalans in a certain forest grove.
sn7.17:1.2 | S: Now at that time the brahmin Bhāradvāja the Builder was having some building work done in that jungle thicket. | T: Now at that time the brahman Navakammika [“Builder”] Bhāradvāja was getting some work done in that forest grove.
sn7.17:1.3 | S: He saw the Buddha sitting down cross-legged at the root of a certain sal tree, his body set straight, and mindfulness brought to the present. | T: He saw the Blessed One sitting under a Sal tree—his legs folded crosswise, his body held erect, with mindfulness set to the fore.
sn7.17:1.4 | S: Seeing this, it occurred to him, | T: On seeing him, the thought occurred to the brahman:
sn7.17:1.5 | S: “I enjoy having this building work done here in the jungle. | T: “Here I am, taking delight in getting work done in this forest grove.
sn7.17:1.6 | S: I wonder what the ascetic Gotama enjoys having done?” | T: But what does this contemplative take delight in getting done?”
sn7.17:1.7 | S: Then Bhāradvāja the Builder went up to the Buddha and addressed him in verse: | T: So he went to the Blessed One and on arrival recited this verse:
sn7.17:2.1 | S: “What kind of works are done by you, | T: “What jobs are getting done,
sn7.17:2.2 | S: a mendicant in the sal jungle? | T: monk in the Sal forest?
sn7.17:2.3 | S: How do you find enjoyment | T: Alone in the wilderness, in what does Gotama find delight?”
sn7.17:2.4 | S: alone in the wilderness, Gotama?” | T: (none)
sn7.17:3.1 | S: “There’s nothing I need do in the jungle; | T: The Buddha: “I have no work to do in the forest.
sn7.17:3.2 | S: my jungle’s cut at the root, it’s withered away. | T: The forest of restless dancing about I’ve cut at the root.
sn7.17:3.3 | S: Free of snarls and thorns in the jungle, | T: Though in the forest, I’m deforested, de-arrowed.
sn7.17:3.4 | S: I enjoy solitude, having given up discontent.” | T: I delight alone, discontent cast away.”
sn7.17:4.1 | S: When he had spoken, the brahmin Bhāradvāja the Builder said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, the brahman Navakammika Bhāradvāja said to the Blessed One:
sn7.17:4.2 | S: “Excellent, worthy Gotama … | T: “Magnificent, Master Gotama! Magnificent! Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has Master Gotama—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear.
sn7.17:4.3 | S: From this day forth, may the worthy Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: I go to Master Gotama for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks. May Master Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge from this day forward, for life.”

## an10.46
an10.46:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans, near Kapilavatthu in the Banyan Tree Monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Kapilavatthu in the Banyan Park.
an10.46:1.2 | S: Then on the sabbath several Sakyan lay followers went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to them: | T: Then many Sakyan lay followers, it being the uposatha day, went to the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to him, they sat to one side. As they were sitting there, the Blessed One said to them,
an10.46:1.3 | S: “Sakyans, do you observe the sabbath with its eight factors?” | T: “Sakyans, do you observe the eight-factored uposatha?”
an10.46:1.4 | S: “Sir, sometimes we do, sometimes we don’t.” | T: “Sometimes we do, lord, and sometimes we don’t.”
an10.46:1.5 | S: “That’s your loss, Sakyans, it’s your misfortune. In this life with its fear of sorrow and death, you sometimes keep the sabbath and you sometimes don’t. | T: “It’s no gain for you, Sakyans. It’s ill-gotten, that in this life so endangered by grief, in this life so endangered by death, you sometimes observe the eight-factored uposatha and sometimes don’t.
an10.46:2.1 | S: What do you think, Sakyans? | T: “What do you think, Sakyans?
an10.46:2.2 | S: Take a man who earns half a dollar for an honest day’s work. | T: Suppose a man, by some profession or other, without encountering an unskillful day, were to earn a half-kahāpaṇa.
an10.46:2.3 | S: Is this enough to call him a deft and industrious man?” | T: Would he deserve to be called a capable man, full of initiative?”
an10.46:2.4 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
an10.46:3.1 | S: “What do you think, Sakyans? | T: (none)
an10.46:3.2 | S: Take a man who earns a dollar for an honest day’s work. | T: “Suppose a man, by some profession or other, without encountering an unskillful day, were to earn a kahāpaṇa…
an10.46:3.3 | S: Is this enough to call him a deft and industrious man?” | T: (none)
an10.46:3.4 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: (none)

