## sn22.82
sn22.82:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in the stilt longhouse of Migāra’s mother in the Eastern Monastery, together with a large Saṅgha of monks. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in the Eastern Monastery, the palace of Migāra’s mother.
sn22.82:1.2 | S: Now, at that time it was the sabbath—the full moon on the fifteenth day—and the Buddha was sitting in the open surrounded by the Saṅgha of monks. | T: And on that occasion—the uposatha of the fifteenth, the night of a very full moon—he was sitting out in the open with the Saṅgha of monks.
sn22.82:2.1 | S: Then one of the monks rose from his seat, arranged his robe over one shoulder, raised his cupped palms toward the Buddha, and said: | T: Then a certain monk, rising from his seat, arranging his robe over one shoulder, and placing his hands palm-to-palm over the heart, said to the Blessed One:
sn22.82:2.2 | S: “Sir, I’d like to ask the Buddha about a certain point, if you’d take the time to answer.” | T: “Venerable sir, there is an area where, if the Blessed One would give me leave, I would like the answer to a question.”
sn22.82:2.3 | S: “Well then, monk, take your own seat and ask what you wish.” | T: “Very well, then, monk. Sit back down in your seat and ask whatever you want.”
sn22.82:2.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” replied that monk. He took his seat and said to the Buddha: | T: Responding to the Blessed One, “Yes, lord,” the monk sat back down in his seat and said to the Blessed One,
sn22.82:2.5 | S: “Sir, are these the five grasping aggregates, that is: | T: “Aren’t these the five clinging-aggregates, i.e.,
sn22.82:2.6 | S: form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness?” | T: the form clinging-aggregate, the feeling clinging-aggregate, the perception clinging-aggregate, the fabrications clinging-aggregate, the consciousness clinging-aggregate.”
sn22.82:3.1 | S: “Yes, they are,” replied the Buddha. | T: “Monk, these are the five clinging-aggregates,
sn22.82:3.2 | P: Seyyathidaṁ—rūpupādānakkhandho …pe… viññāṇupādānakkhandho”ti. | T: i.e., the form clinging-aggregate, the feeling clinging-aggregate, the perception clinging-aggregate, the fabrications clinging-aggregate, the consciousness clinging-aggregate.”
sn22.82:3.3 | S: Saying “Good, sir”, that monk approved and agreed with what the Buddha said. Then he asked another question: | T: Saying, “Very good, lord,” the monk delighted & approved of the Blessed One’s words and then asked him a further question:
sn22.82:4.1 | S: “But sir, what is the root of these five grasping aggregates?” | T: “But in what, lord, are these five clinging-aggregates rooted?”
sn22.82:4.2 | S: “These five grasping aggregates are rooted in desire.” … | T: “Monk, these five clinging-aggregates are rooted in desire.” Saying, “Very good, lord,” the monk… asked him a further question:
sn22.82:4.3 | S: “But sir, is that grasping the exact same thing as the five grasping aggregates? Or is grasping one thing and the five grasping aggregates another?” | T: “Is clinging the same thing as the five clinging-aggregates, or is clinging separate from the five clinging-aggregates?”
sn22.82:4.4 | S: “Neither. Rather, the desire and greed for them is the grasping there.” | T: “Monk, clinging is neither the same thing as the five clinging-aggregates, nor is it separate from the five clinging-aggregates. Just that whatever passion & delight is there, that’s the clinging there.”
sn22.82:4.5 | S: Saying “Good, sir”, that monk asked another question: | T: Saying, “Very good, lord,” the monk… asked him a further question:
sn22.82:5.1 | S: “But sir, can there be disparity in desire and greed for the five grasping aggregates?” | T: “Might there be diversity in the desire & passion for the five clinging-aggregates?”
sn22.82:5.2 | S: “There can,” said the Buddha. | T: “There might, monk.
sn22.82:5.3 | S: “It’s when someone thinks: | T: There is the case where the thought occurs to someone,
sn22.82:5.4 | S: ‘In the future, may I be of such form, such feeling, such perception, such choices, and such consciousness!’ | T: ‘May I be one with such a form in the future. May I be one with such a feeling… perception… fabrications… such a consciousness in the future.
sn22.82:5.5 | S: That’s how there can be disparity in desire and greed for the five grasping aggregates.” | T: This is how there would be diversity in the desire & passion for the five clinging-aggregates.”
sn22.82:5.6 | S: Saying “Good, sir”, that monk asked another question: | T: Saying, “Very good, lord,” the monk… asked him a further question:
sn22.82:6.1 | S: “Sir, what is the scope of the term ‘aggregates’ as applied to the aggregates?” | T: “To what extent does the designation ‘aggregate’ apply to the aggregates?”
sn22.82:6.2 | S: “Any kind of form at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: this is called the aggregate of form. | T: “Monk, whatever form is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: That is called the form aggregate.
sn22.82:6.3 | S: Any kind of feeling at all … | T: Whatever feeling is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: That is called the feeling aggregate.
sn22.82:6.4 | S: Any kind of perception at all … | T: Whatever perception is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: That is called the perception aggregate.
sn22.82:6.5 | S: Any kind of choices at all … | T: Whatever fabrications are past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: Those are called the fabrications aggregate.
sn22.82:6.6 | S: Any kind of consciousness at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: this is called the aggregate of consciousness. | T: Whatever consciousness is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: That is called the consciousness aggregate.
sn22.82:6.7 | S: That’s the scope of the term ‘aggregates’ as applied to the aggregates.” | T: This is the extent to which the term ‘aggregate’ applies to the aggregates.”
sn22.82:6.8 | S: Saying “Good, sir”, that monk asked another question: | T: Saying, “Very good, lord,” the monk… asked him a further question:
sn22.82:7.1 | S: “What is the cause, sir, what is the reason why the aggregate of form is found? | T: “Lord, what is the cause, what the condition, for the delineation of the form aggregate?
sn22.82:7.2 | S: What is the cause, what is the reason why the aggregate of feeling … | T: What is the cause, what the condition, for the delineation of the feeling aggregate…
sn22.82:7.3 | S: perception … | T: the perception aggregate…
sn22.82:7.4 | S: choices … | T: the fabrications aggregate…
sn22.82:7.5 | S: consciousness is found?” | T: the consciousness aggregate?”
sn22.82:7.6 | S: “The four principal states are the reason why the aggregate of form is found. | T: “Monk, the four great existents [earth, water, fire, & wind] are the cause, the four great existents the condition, for the delineation of the form aggregate.
sn22.82:7.7 | S: Contact is the reason why the aggregates of feeling, | T: Contact is the cause, contact the condition, for the delineation of the feeling aggregate.
sn22.82:7.8 | S: perception, | T: Contact is the cause, contact the condition, for the delineation of the perception aggregate.
sn22.82:7.9 | S: and choices are found. | T: Contact is the cause, contact the condition, for the delineation of the fabrications aggregate.
sn22.82:7.10 | S: Name and form are the reasons why the aggregate of consciousness is found.” | T: Name-&-form is the cause, name-&-form the condition, for the delineation of the consciousness aggregate.”
sn22.82:7.11 | S: Saying “Good, sir”, that monk asked another question: | T: Saying, “Very good, lord,” the monk… asked him a further question:
sn22.82:8.1 | S: “Sir, how does substantialist view come about?” | T: “Lord, how does self-identification view come about?”
sn22.82:8.2 | S: “It’s when an unlearned ordinary person has not seen the noble ones, and is neither skilled nor trained in the teaching of the noble ones. They’ve not seen true persons, and are neither skilled nor trained in the teaching of the true persons. | T: “There is the case, monk, where an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person—who has no regard for noble ones, is not well-versed or disciplined in their Dhamma; who has no regard for people of integrity, is not well-versed or disciplined in their Dhamma—
sn22.82:8.3 | S: They regard form as self, self as having form, form in self, or self in form. | T: assumes form to be the self, or the self as possessing form, or form as in the self, or the self as in form.
sn22.82:8.4 | S: They regard feeling … | T: “He assumes feeling to be the self, or the self as possessing feeling, or feeling as in the self, or the self as in feeling.
sn22.82:8.5 | S: perception … | T: He assumes perception to be the self, or the self as possessing perception, or perception as in the self, or the self as in perception.
sn22.82:8.6 | S: choices … | T: He assumes fabrications to be the self, or the self as possessing fabrications, or fabrications as in the self, or the self as in fabrications.
sn22.82:8.7 | S: consciousness as self, self as having consciousness, consciousness in self, or self in consciousness. | T: He assumes consciousness to be the self, or the self as possessing consciousness, or consciousness as in the self, or the self as in consciousness.
sn22.82:8.8 | S: That’s how substantialist view comes about.” | T: “This, monk, is how self-identification view comes about.”
sn22.82:8.9 | S: Saying “Good, sir”, that monk … asked another question: | T: Saying, “Very good, lord,” the monk… asked him a further question:
sn22.82:9.1 | S: “But sir, how does substantialist view not come about?” | T: “Lord, how does self-identification view no longer come about?”
sn22.82:9.2 | S: “It’s when a learned noble disciple has seen the noble ones, and is skilled and trained in the teaching of the noble ones. They’ve seen true persons, and are skilled and trained in the teaching of the true persons. | T: “There is the case, monk, where a well-instructed disciple of the noble ones—who has regard for noble ones, is well-versed & disciplined in their Dhamma; who has regard for people of integrity, is well-versed & disciplined in their Dhamma—
sn22.82:9.3 | S: They don’t regard form as self, self as having form, form in self, or self in form. | T: doesn’t assume form to be the self, or the self as possessing form, or form as in the self, or the self as in form.
sn22.82:9.4 | S: They don’t regard feeling … | T: He doesn’t assume feeling to be the self.…
sn22.82:9.5 | S: perception … | T: doesn’t assume perception to be the self.…
sn22.82:9.6 | S: choices … | T: doesn’t assume fabrications to be the self.…
sn22.82:9.7 | S: consciousness as self, self as having consciousness, consciousness in self, or self in consciousness. | T: He doesn’t assume consciousness to be the self, or the self as possessing consciousness, or consciousness as in the self, or the self as in consciousness.
sn22.82:9.8 | S: That’s how substantialist view does not come about.” | T: “This, monk, is how self-identification view no longer comes about.”
sn22.82:9.9 | S: Saying “Good, sir”, that monk … asked another question: | T: Saying, “Very good, lord,” the monk… asked him a further question:
sn22.82:10.1 | S: “Sir, what’s the gratification, the drawback, and the escape when it comes to form, | T: “What, lord, is the allure of form? What is its drawback? What is the escape from it?
sn22.82:10.2 | S: feeling, | T: What is the allure of feeling…
sn22.82:10.3 | S: perception, | T: perception…
sn22.82:10.4 | S: choices, | T: fabrications…
sn22.82:10.5 | S: and consciousness?” | T: consciousness? What is its drawback? What is the escape from it?”
sn22.82:10.6 | S: “The pleasure and happiness that arise from form: this is its gratification. | T: “Monk, whatever pleasure & joy arises dependent on form: that is the allure of form.
sn22.82:10.7 | S: That form is impermanent, suffering, and perishable: this is its drawback. | T: The fact that form is inconstant, stressful, subject to change: that is the drawback of form.
sn22.82:10.8 | S: Removing and giving up desire and greed for form: this is its escape. | T: The subduing of desire & passion, the abandoning of desire & passion for form: That is the escape from form.
sn22.82:10.9 | S: The pleasure and happiness that arise from feeling … | T: “Whatever pleasure & joy arises dependent on feeling: That is the allure of feeling.…
sn22.82:10.10 | S: perception … | T: “Whatever pleasure & joy arises dependent on perception: That is the allure of perception.…
sn22.82:10.11 | S: choices … | T: “Whatever pleasure & joy arises dependent on fabrications: That is the allure of fabrications.…
sn22.82:10.12 | S: consciousness: this is its gratification. | T: “Whatever pleasure & joy arises dependent on consciousness: that is the allure of consciousness.
sn22.82:10.13 | S: That consciousness is impermanent, suffering, and perishable: this is its drawback. | T: The fact that consciousness is inconstant, stressful, subject to change: that is the drawback of consciousness.
sn22.82:10.14 | S: Removing and giving up desire and greed for consciousness: this is its escape.” | T: The subduing of desire & passion, the abandoning of desire & passion for consciousness: That is the escape from consciousness.”
sn22.82:10.15 | S: Saying “Good, sir”, that monk approved and agreed with what the Buddha said. Then he asked another question: | T: Saying, “Very good, lord,” the monk… asked him a further question:
sn22.82:11.1 | S: “Sir, how does one know and see so that there’s no I-making, mine-making, or underlying tendency to conceit for this conscious body and externally for all signs?” | T: “Knowing in what way, seeing in what way, is there—with regard to this body endowed with consciousness, and with regard to all external signs—no longer any I-making, or my-making, or obsession with conceit?”
sn22.82:11.2 | S: “One truly sees any kind of form at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> form—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “Monk, one sees any form whatsoever—past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near—every form, as it has come to be with right discernment: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
sn22.82:11.3 | S: One truly sees any kind of feeling … | T: “One sees any feeling whatsoever…
sn22.82:11.4 | S: perception … | T: any perception whatsoever…
sn22.82:11.5 | S: choices … | T: any fabrications whatsoever…
sn22.82:11.6 | S: consciousness at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: <em>all</em> consciousness—with right understanding: ‘This is not mine, I am not this, this is not my self.’ | T: “One sees any consciousness whatsoever—past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near—every consciousness—as it has come to be with right discernment: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’”
sn22.82:11.7 | S: That’s how to know and see so that there’s no I-making, mine-making, or underlying tendency to conceit for this conscious body and externally for all signs.” | T: “Monk, knowing in this way, seeing in this way there is—with regard to this body endowed with consciousness, and with regard to all external signs—no longer any I-making, or my-making, or obsession with conceit.”
sn22.82:12.1 | S: Now at that time one of the monks had the thought: | T: Now at that moment this line of thinking appeared in the awareness of a certain monk:
sn22.82:12.2 | S: “So it seems, worthy sir, that form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness are not-self. | T: “So—form is not-self, feeling is not-self, perception is not-self, fabrications are not-self, consciousness is not-self.
sn22.82:12.3 | S: Then how will the deeds done by not-self affect the self?” | T: Then what self will be touched by the actions done by what is not-self?”
sn22.82:12.4 | S: Then the Buddha, knowing that monk’s train of thought, addressed the monks: | T: Then the Blessed One, realizing with his awareness the line of thinking in that monk’s awareness, addressed the monks:
sn22.82:13.1 | S: “It’s possible that some futile person here—unknowing and ignorant, his mind dominated by craving—thinks he can overstep the Teacher’s instructions. He thinks: | T: “It’s possible that a senseless person—immersed in ignorance, overcome with craving—might think that he could outsmart the Teacher’s message in this way:
sn22.82:13.2 | S: ‘So it seems, worthy sir, that form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness are not-self. | T: ‘So—form is not-self, feeling is not-self, perception is not-self, fabrications are not-self, consciousness is not-self.
sn22.82:13.3 | S: Then how will the deeds done by not-self affect the self?’ | T: Then what self will be touched by the actions done by what is not-self?’
sn22.82:13.4 | S: Now, monks, you have been educated by me in questioning with regard to all these things in all such cases. | T: “Now, monks, haven’t I trained you in counter-questioning with regard to this & that topic here & there?
sn22.82:14.1 | S: What do you think, monks? | T: What do you think?
sn22.82:14.2 | S: Is form permanent or impermanent?” | T: Is form constant or inconstant?”—
sn22.82:14.3 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” | T: “Inconstant, lord.”—“And is that which is inconstant easeful or stressful?”—“Stressful, lord.”—“And is it fitting to regard what is inconstant, stressful, subject to change as: ‘This is mine. This is my self. This is what I am’?” “No, lord.”
sn22.82:14.4 | S: “Is feeling … | T: “… Is feeling constant or inconstant?”—“Inconstant, lord.” …
sn22.82:14.5 | S: perception … | T: “… Is perception constant or inconstant?”—“Inconstant, lord.” …
sn22.82:14.6 | S: choices … | T: “… Are fabrications constant or inconstant?”—“Inconstant, lord.” …
sn22.82:14.7 | S: consciousness permanent or impermanent?” | T: “What do you think, monks? Is consciousness constant or inconstant?”—
sn22.82:14.8 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” | T: “Inconstant, lord.”—
sn22.82:14.9 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, is it suffering or happiness?” | T: “And is that which is inconstant easeful or stressful?”—
sn22.82:14.10 | S: “Suffering, sir.” | T: “Stressful, lord.”—
sn22.82:14.11 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable, is it fit to be regarded thus: | T: “And is it fitting to regard what is inconstant, stressful, subject to change as:
sn22.82:14.12 | S: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self’?” | T: ‘This is mine. This is my self. This is what I am’?”
sn22.82:14.13 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
sn22.82:14.14 | S: “So you should truly see … | T: “Thus, monks, any form whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: Every form is to be seen as it has come to be with right discernment as: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’ “Any feeling whatsoever… “Any perception whatsoever… “Any fabrications whatsoever… “Any consciousness whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: Every consciousness is to be seen as it has come to be with right discernment as: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
sn22.82:14.15 | S: Seeing this … | T: “Seeing thus, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with form, disenchanted with feeling, disenchanted with perception, disenchanted with fabrications, disenchanted with consciousness. Disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate. Through dispassion, he is released. With release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released.’
sn22.82:14.16 | S: They understand: ‘… there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: He discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’”
sn22.82:15.1 | S: “Two on the aggregates; exactly the same; <j>and can there be; | T: (none)
sn22.82:15.2 | S: on the term; and on the cause; | T: (none)
sn22.82:15.3 | S: two questions on substantial reality; | T: (none)
sn22.82:15.4 | S: gratification; and that with consciousness: | T: (none)
sn22.82:15.5 | S: these are the ten questions | T: (none)
sn22.82:15.6 | S: the monk came to ask.” | T: (none)
sn22.82:15.7 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
sn22.82:15.8 | P: Khajjanīyavaggo tatiyo. | T: (none)
sn22.82:16.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn22.82:16.1 | P: Assādo dve samudayā, | T: (none)
sn22.82:16.2 | P: arahantehi apare dve; | T: (none)
sn22.82:16.3 | P: Sīho khajjanī piṇḍolyaṁ, | T: (none)
sn22.82:16.4 | P: pālileyyena puṇṇamāti. | T: (none)


